2026-10-16T03:40:46.8617089Z   ISSUE_NUMBER: 55935
2026-10-16T03:40:46.8617576Z   ISSUE_TITLE: plugin: shipped @opencode-ai/plugin package is stale and omits context fields (storage) provided by the runtime
2026-10-16T03:40:46.8622429Z   ISSUE_BODY: ## Summary

The `@opencode-ai/plugin` package that opencode generates into `.opencode/` is pinned to 1.18.30 while the runtime is 2.0.28. Its plugin context type omits runtime-provided capabilities (e.g. `storage`), so a plugin written against the shipped description cannot see features the runtime actually passes to it.

## Environment

- opencode version: 2.0.28
- OS: Linux 7.0.0-38-generic x86_64
- Terminal: TERM_PROGRAM=vscode, TERM=xterm-256color, COLORTERM=truecolor
- Shell: /bin/bash
- Install/channel: release build at `~/.opencode/bin/opencode`
- Active plugins: 5 local plugins under `.opencode/plugins/` (agentgate.js, instructions.js, limits.js, readonly.js, taskgate.js)

## Reproduction

1. Start opencode in a fresh workspace; it generates `.opencode/package.json` with `"@opencode-ai/plugin": "1.18.30"` and installs it under `.opencode/node_modules/`.
2. Inspect the package exports: it ships `./v2/effect` and `./v2/promise` entrypoints whose `PluginContext` declares `options`, `agent`, `aisdk`, `catalog`, `command`, `integration`, `plugin`, `reference`, `skill` — and no `storage`.
3. Write a plugin that uses `ctx.storage` as the running opencode instance provides it.

## Expected Behavior

The generated `@opencode-ai/plugin` package should match the running opencode version, so its types and entrypoints describe exactly the context and capabilities the runtime passes to plugins (including `storage`).

## Actual Behavior

The shipped package is a stale 1.18.30 while the runtime is 2.0.28. `PluginContext` in `.opencode/node_modules/@opencode-ai/plugin/dist/v2/effect/context.d.ts` has no `storage` member (no `storage`/`persist`/`kv` reference exists anywhere in its `dist/`), yet the runtime supplies it — the binary contains `effect({...storage:...})` and `storage` typed onto the plugin context. A plugin typed against the package therefore cannot reference a capability that exists at runtime; it either fails typecheck or must cast to `any`.

## Additional Context

- `.opencode/package.json`, `.opencode/package-lock.json` and `.opencode/bun.lock` are generated by opencode; our project test declares them generated and forbids touching them, so we cannot fix the pin locally.
- Our plugins do not import this package (they import `effect` directly), so the mismatch is currently latent for us; it is reported as a version/description divergence.
- Reproducible consistently (the generated pin and package contents are stable across starts).

